  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "KF9IwMikjjBy+cK4ijjXSsAlpJM=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:27:20.371Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Nextcloud syncs and shares files, and its activity feed reflects exactly that: a stream of file events. A document being signed off and a document being moved produce entries of the same kind, so the feed cannot distinguish a decision from a filing action.\n\nThis is a sharper version of the general document-management gap. The system is not missing a log; it has a detailed one. What it is missing is a type distinction between events that change where a file is and events that change what the organisation has committed to. Powerhouse records the decision the file represents, which is a different object from the file.\n\nThree leads carry this finding \u2014 in FR, DE and CH, all at \"3 \u00b7 later\". For partners who deploy Nextcloud as a sovereign alternative to hosted file services, the gap is worth naming precisely, because the usual objection is that the activity feed already covers it.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (3)\n\n**3 \u00b7 later**\n- Arawa (FR) \u2014 https://arawa.fr\n- Compaso GmbH (DE) \u2014 https://compaso.de\n- Linuxfabrik GmbH (CH Zurich) \u2014 https://linuxfabrik.ch\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> Nextcloud syncs and shares files; its activity feed is a file-event stream, not a business record. A signed-off document and a moved document look the same to it. Powerhouse records the decision the file represents.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:27:20.371800Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:27:20.371804Z"
    }
  },
